# Introduction # {#intro}

Sensor observations, positioning data, measurements, mobility information... are commonly published as time series: a timestamp with one or more values.
Representing time series in RDF drastically raises the verbosity, as each reading is often given its own identifier, for which contextual information is repeated on all readings.
With RDF Time Series Snippets, a publisher keeps the readings compact and describes them with RDF:

- A [=Snippet=] describes a table of readings: every [=Point=] has one time and values for one or more [=Fields=]. Series that are measured together share one [=Time Field=].
- The readings are either embedded in the metadata, as one small node per point (`tss:pointOf`), or kept in an external data file (`tss:dataFile`). The same field descriptions read both.
- Each field states the column name and datatype of its values and, optionally, what they observe, in which unit, with which sensor, of which feature of interest.
- A data file can give the time of each row as a step on a regular [=Time Grid=], and state the values of a field as differences from a baseline (`tss:valueEncoding`).

The metadata also states the [=Time Window=] of the readings, whether they may still change, when the snippet and its data were last checked to be up to date, and an optional [=Stored Summary=] of each field.

## Namespaces ## {#namespaces}

RDF Time Series Snippets use the following prefix and namespace:

```turtle
@prefix tss: <https://w3id.org/tss#> .
```

## Embedded example ## {#embedded-example}

This example is a complete [=Embedded Snippet=]: five conductivity readings of one sensor, embedded in the metadata.

<div class="example" id="example-embedded-snippet">
```turtle
@prefix tss: <https://w3id.org/tss#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix quantitykind: <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/> .
@prefix unit: <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/> .
@prefix ex: <https://example.org/ex#> .

ex:snippet
    a tss:Snippet ;
    rdfs:label "Conductivity readings"@en ;
    tss:embedded true ;
    tss:mutable true ;
    tss:timeDatatype xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:committedUntil "2024-05-12T09:46:10Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:producedBy ex:producer ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeField ex:time-field ;
    tss:key ex:time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:time-field,
        ex:value-field .

ex:time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    rdfs:label "Observation time"@en ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    rdfs:label "Electrical conductivity"@en ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:minimum "0"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:maximum "10000"^^xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    tss:unit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM ;
    tss:sensor ex:sensor-1 ;
    tss:featureOfInterest ex:feature-1 .

[] tss:pointOf ex:snippet ;
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ex:value-field "4810.5"^^xsd:double .

[] tss:pointOf ex:snippet ;
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ex:value-field "4722.4"^^xsd:double .

[] tss:pointOf ex:snippet ;
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T08:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ex:value-field "4751.9"^^xsd:double .

[] tss:pointOf ex:snippet ;
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T09:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ex:value-field "4719.3"^^xsd:double .

[] tss:pointOf ex:snippet ;
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T09:45:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ex:value-field "4783.6"^^xsd:double .
```
</div>

Each point links to the snippet with `tss:pointOf`, and the IRIs of the fields are the predicates of its values. Expanded into observations ([[#expanding]]), every value of a [=Measured Field=] becomes one observation, with a link back to the snippet, the time of its point and what its field says. Written with SOSA [[VOCAB-SSN]] and `dcterms:source`, the first two observations are:

<div class="example" id="example-observations">
```turtle
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix qudt: <http://qudt.org/schema/qudt/> .

[] a sosa:Observation ;
    dcterms:source ex:snippet ;
    sosa:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    sosa:madeBySensor ex:sensor-1 ;
    sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ex:feature-1 ;
    qudt:hasUnit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM ;
    sosa:resultTime "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    sosa:hasSimpleResult "4810.5"^^xsd:double .

[] a sosa:Observation ;
    dcterms:source ex:snippet ;
    sosa:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    sosa:madeBySensor ex:sensor-1 ;
    sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ex:feature-1 ;
    qudt:hasUnit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM ;
    sosa:resultTime "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    sosa:hasSimpleResult "4722.4"^^xsd:double .

# ... and three more observations, one for each other point
```
</div>

The examples that follow reuse the prefixes of these two examples.

# Definitions # {#definitions}

A <dfn>Time Series</dfn> is a set of data points ordered by time, where each data point consists of a timestamp and a value.

A <dfn>Snippet</dfn> is a table of readings that share one time field, described by one `tss:Snippet`: each row is a [=Point=], each column a [=Field=]. Each field other than the [=Time Field=] holds one [=Time Series=].

A <dfn>Field</dfn> is one column of a [=Snippet=], described by one `tss:Field`: the name and datatype of its values and, optionally, what they mean.

A <dfn>Point</dfn> is one row of a [=Snippet=]: one time, and at most one value for each [=Field=].

The <dfn>Time Field</dfn> of a snippet is the field that holds the time of each point (`tss:timeField`).

The <dfn>Key</dfn> of a snippet is the field, or the fields, whose values identify a point (`tss:key`). Usually the key is the time field.

The <dfn>Time Window</dfn> of a snippet is the period from `tss:from` (included) until `tss:until` (not included). A point lies in the window when its time is not before `tss:from` and before `tss:until`. A bound that is not stated leaves that side of the window open.

An <dfn>Embedded Snippet</dfn> keeps its points in the metadata, as nodes that link to it with `tss:pointOf` (`tss:embedded true`). An <dfn>External Snippet</dfn> keeps them in a data file, `tss:dataFile` (`tss:embedded false`).

The <dfn id="dfn-time-grid">Time Grid</dfn> of an external snippet is a series of times at regular steps: step 0 at `tss:timeBase`, and every next step `tss:timeStep` later ([[#time-grid]]).

The <dfn>Step Field</dfn> of an external snippet with a time grid is the field whose column holds the step of each row (`tss:stepField`).

An <dfn>Empty Value</dfn> is the absence of a value: an empty CSV cell, a Parquet NULL, an embedded point without a value for the field, or the empty text `""`. A text that holds only spaces is not empty.

The <dfn>Decoded Value</dfn> of a value is what the value stands for: the value itself, or, for a field with a `tss:valueEncoding`, the value decoded as its encoding says ([[#value-encoding]]).

A <dfn>Measured Field</dfn> is a field with a `tss:observedProperty` that is not the time field. Each of its values is an observation.

A <dfn>Describing Field</dfn> says something about the values of another field (`tss:describesField`).

A <dfn>Stored Summary</dfn> holds numbers computed from the values of one field, such as their count and mean (`tss:summary`).

A <dfn>Write</dfn> is a change to the points of a snippet, such as adding a point.

# The snippet # {#the-snippet}

A metadata document MUST describe exactly one `tss:Snippet`. The snippet has one time field, which gives the time of every point for all its other fields. The table lists its properties. "Required" means exactly one value unless the table says otherwise, "optional" at most one, and "not allowed" none.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>External snippet</th><th>Embedded snippet</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:embedded</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>required: <code>false</code></td><td>required: <code>true</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:mutable</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>required</td><td>required</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeDatatype</code></td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td><td>required</td><td>required</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:committedUntil</code></td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td><td>required</td><td>required</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasField</code></td><td>a <code>tss:Field</code></td><td>required, one or more</td><td>required, one or more</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeField</code></td><td>one of its fields</td><td>required</td><td>required</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:key</code></td><td>one of its fields</td><td>required, one or more</td><td>required, one or more</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:from</code></td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:until</code></td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code>, after <code>tss:from</code></td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:dataFile</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>required</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:format</code></td><td><code>tss:CSV</code> or <code>tss:Parquet</code></td><td>required</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:delimiter</code></td><td>one character</td><td>optional, for <code>tss:CSV</code>; default <code>","</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:encoding</code></td><td>text, such as <code>"utf-8"</code></td><td>optional, for <code>tss:CSV</code>; default <code>"utf-8"</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:header</code></td><td><code>xsd:boolean</code></td><td>optional, for <code>tss:CSV</code>; default <code>true</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeBase</code></td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td><td>optional, together with <code>tss:timeStep</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeStep</code></td><td><code>xsd:duration</code>, more than zero</td><td>optional, together with <code>tss:timeBase</code></td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:stepField</code></td><td>one of its fields</td><td>required with a time grid; not allowed without one</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:producedBy</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>rdfs:label</code></td><td>literal</td><td>optional, any number</td><td>optional, any number</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:snippetDescription</code></td><td>a node, any vocabulary</td><td>optional</td><td>optional</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## Where the readings are ## {#storage}

`tss:embedded` says where the points of the snippet are:

- `true`: in the metadata itself, as nodes that link to the snippet with `tss:pointOf` ([[#points]]). Every point states its own time and its readings themselves. The snippet MUST NOT state `tss:dataFile`, `tss:format`, `tss:delimiter`, `tss:encoding` or `tss:header`, which describe a data file, nor `tss:timeBase`, `tss:timeStep` or `tss:stepField`, which describe a [=Time Grid=] ([[#time-grid]]). Its fields MUST NOT state a value encoding ([[#value-encoding]]).
- `false`: in the data file `tss:dataFile`, read as `tss:format` says ([[#data-files]]). No node links to an external snippet with `tss:pointOf`.

A consumer selects the rules by this one boolean. Metadata without exactly one boolean `tss:embedded` does not say where its readings are.

## Time ## {#time}

### Times ### {#times}

Every time of a snippet is an `xsd:dateTime` [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]]: a date and a time of day, such as `"2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"`. The times of a snippet are:

- on the snippet: `tss:committedUntil`, `tss:from`, `tss:until` and `tss:timeBase`;
- on a [=Stored Summary=]: `tss:from`, `tss:until`, `tss:firstTime`, `tss:lastTime` and `tss:generatedAtTime`;
- in each point: its value for the [=Time Field=].

A snippet states the datatype of its times with exactly one `tss:timeDatatype`, which MUST be `xsd:dateTime`. The `tss:datatype` of the time field MUST be `xsd:dateTime` too. Times are compared as the moments they stand for: two times that are the same moment are the same time.

### The time field and the key ### {#time-and-key}

`tss:timeField` names the [=Time Field=]. It MUST be one of the snippet's own fields (`tss:hasField`). Every point MUST have a time: its value for the time field. In a data file with a [=Time Grid=], the time of a row is the time of its step ([[#time-grid]]).

`tss:key` names the field or fields whose values identify a point. A snippet MUST state at least one key field, and each key field MUST be one of the snippet's own fields. No two points may have the same values for all key fields; two times that are the same moment are the same value. In all the examples, the key is the time field.

### The time window ### {#window}

`tss:from` and `tss:until` state the [=Time Window=] of the snippet: the readings lie in [`tss:from`, `tss:until`), so a point at exactly `tss:until` is not in the window. Both are times of the snippet ([[#times]]), and `tss:until` MUST come after `tss:from`. The time of every point of an embedded snippet MUST lie in the window. Consumers of an external snippet use only the rows inside the window: the data file may hold more.

Note: The window does not have to be tight: `tss:until` may lie after the newest point, as in <a href="#example-embedded-snippet">the embedded example</a>.

## Changes ## {#change}

- `tss:mutable` says whether the data of the snippet may still change. `false` means it never changes: no point is added, changed or removed. `true` means points may be added ([[#updating]]).
- `tss:committedUntil` is the last time the snippet and its data were checked to be up to date. Every [=Write=] MUST set it to the time of that write; a check that finds nothing new may move it forward too. It is not the time of a reading and no bound of the [=Time Window=]: it may lie after the newest point, and after `tss:until`.

For example, when the newest point is a month old and `tss:committedUntil` is yesterday, the snippet and its data are up to date: no new readings came in between the newest point and yesterday.

Each [=Stored Summary=] states when it was computed with its own `tss:generatedAtTime` ([[#summaries]]).

## Description and provenance ## {#provenance}

- `tss:producedBy` is the agent that produced the data, as one IRI.
- `rdfs:label` names the snippet for people.
- `tss:snippetDescription` points to a node that describes the snippet in any vocabulary, for example where it is:

<div class="example" id="example-snippet-description">
```turtle
ex:snippet
    tss:snippetDescription [
        dcterms:spatial [
            a dcterms:Location ;
            rdfs:label "Site A"@en
        ]
    ] .
```
</div>

# Fields # {#fields}

Each [=Field=] is a `tss:Field`, linked from exactly one snippet with `tss:hasField`. The same field description reads a CSV column, a Parquet column and the values of the embedded points. In an embedded point, the IRI of a field is the predicate of its values, so the fields of an embedded snippet are IRIs.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnName</code></td><td>non-empty text</td><td>required; none for the time field of a snippet with a [=Time Grid=]</td><td>the name of the field's column (the CSV header, the Parquet column); unique within the snippet</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:datatype</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>required</td><td>the datatype of the values, such as <code>xsd:double</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnIndex</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>optional; none for the time field of a snippet with a [=Time Grid=]</td><td>the position of the column, the first being 0; unique within the snippet</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:language</code></td><td>language tag</td><td>optional</td><td>the language of the values of an <code>rdf:langString</code> field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:minimum</code>, <code>tss:maximum</code></td><td>number</td><td>optional</td><td>the allowed range of the [=Decoded Values=], bounds included; the maximum is not below the minimum</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:valueEncoding</code></td><td>IRI, such as <code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code></td><td>optional, for a field of an external snippet</td><td>how the values in the data file are stated when they are not the readings themselves ([[#value-encoding]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:baseline</code></td><td>number</td><td>with <code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code> only</td><td>the number that each value is the difference from ([[#value-encoding]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedProperty</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>what the values observe; makes the field a [=Measured Field=]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:unit</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>the unit of the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:sensor</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>the sensor that made the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:featureOfInterest</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>optional</td><td>the thing the values are about</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:describesField</code></td><td>a field of the same snippet</td><td>optional, any number</td><td>the field whose values this field says something about</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:summary</code></td><td>a node</td><td>optional</td><td>a [=Stored Summary=] of the values ([[#summaries]])</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>rdfs:label</code>, <code>rdfs:comment</code></td><td>literal</td><td>optional, any number</td><td>for people</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:fieldDescription</code></td><td>a node, any vocabulary</td><td>optional</td><td>a free description of the field</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

## Values and datatypes ## {#datatypes}

Every value is a text: a CSV cell, the lexical form of a literal in an embedded point, or a Parquet value written in the lexical form of its type ([[#parquet]]). `tss:datatype` is the logical datatype that reads that text; the data file keeps its own types. In an embedded point, every value MUST be a literal whose datatype is the field's `tss:datatype`. A value MUST be in the lexical form of its datatype [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]]. Implementations check at least these forms:

- `xsd:double`, `xsd:float` and `xsd:decimal`; `xsd:integer`, `xsd:long`, `xsd:int`, `xsd:short`, `xsd:byte`, `xsd:nonNegativeInteger`, `xsd:positiveInteger`, `xsd:nonPositiveInteger`, `xsd:negativeInteger` and the unsigned types;
- `xsd:boolean` (`true`, `false`, `1`, `0`);
- `xsd:dateTime`, `xsd:dateTimeStamp` and `xsd:date`, which must also be real days: 30 February is no date.

Any other datatype, such as `xsd:string`, accepts any text. A value keeps its text exactly as it is written: `" 12 "` is not read as 12, so a check can report it. The numeric datatypes are `xsd:double`, `xsd:float`, `xsd:decimal` and the XSD integer types: only a numeric field has an observed minimum and maximum, a mean and a median in its [=Stored Summary=].

An `rdf:langString` field states the language of its values with `tss:language`.

## What a field observes ## {#meaning}

`tss:observedProperty`, `tss:unit`, `tss:sensor` and `tss:featureOfInterest` say what the values of a field mean. They are stated field by field, so the fields of one snippet can observe different properties, with different sensors, in different units. When the snippet is expanded ([[#expanding]]), they are what each observation of the field holds.

`tss:minimum` and `tss:maximum` are the allowed range of a numeric field, not the range of the values that occur: those are in the stored summary (`tss:observedMinimum`, `tss:observedMaximum`).

## Describing fields ## {#describing}

A [=Describing Field=] says something about the values of another field of the same snippet, in the same point. It states that field with `tss:describesField`.

<div class="example" id="example-describing-field">
Two series in one embedded snippet: a value, and a status that describes it.

```turtle
ex:two-series-snippet
    a tss:Snippet ;
    tss:embedded true ;
    tss:mutable false ;
    tss:timeDatatype xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:committedUntil "2024-05-12T09:46:10Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeField ex:two-series-time-field ;
    tss:key ex:two-series-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:two-series-time-field,
        ex:two-series-value-field,
        ex:two-series-status-field .

ex:two-series-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:two-series-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:observedProperty quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
    tss:unit unit:MicroS-PER-CentiM .

ex:two-series-status-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "status" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:string ;
    tss:describesField ex:two-series-value-field .

[] tss:pointOf ex:two-series-snippet ;
    ex:two-series-time-field "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ex:two-series-value-field "4810.5"^^xsd:double ;
    ex:two-series-status-field "ok"^^xsd:string .

[] tss:pointOf ex:two-series-snippet ;
    ex:two-series-time-field "2024-05-12T08:15:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ex:two-series-value-field "4722.4"^^xsd:double ;
    ex:two-series-status-field "estimated"^^xsd:string .
```
</div>

A describing field with its own `tss:observedProperty` is also a [=Measured Field=]: its values become observations of their own.

`tss:fieldDescription`, like `tss:snippetDescription`, points to a node that describes the field in any vocabulary:

<div class="example" id="example-field-description">
```turtle
ex:value-field
    tss:fieldDescription [
        dcterms:description "Electrical conductivity"@en ;
        dcterms:subject quantitykind:ElectricConductivity ;
        dcterms:relation ex:sensor-1, ex:feature-1
    ] .
```
</div>

# Embedded points # {#points}

An [=Embedded Snippet=] keeps its points in the metadata, as RDF. A [=Point=] is a node that links to its snippet with exactly one `tss:pointOf`. Every node with a `tss:pointOf` is a point: its type follows from that link, so a point states no `rdf:type`. It needs no identifier either, so it is usually a blank node.

- Each value of a point is one triple: the point is the subject, the IRI of the field the predicate, and the object a literal with the field's `tss:datatype` ([[#datatypes]]).
- A point has at most one value for each field.
- A point has no triple for a field it has no value for: that is an [=Empty Value=].
- Every point has a value for the time field ([[#time-and-key]]), and states its readings themselves: an embedded snippet has no [=Time Grid=] and no value encoding.
- Every value of a field of the snippet MUST be in a point of that snippet.
- Every property of a point SHOULD be `tss:pointOf` or a field of its snippet. Values of other properties are not values of the snippet.
- The points have no order. A consumer orders them by their time.

A snippet may have no points yet. No property of a point has a meaning of its own: the fields say what each value means.

# External data files # {#data-files}

An [=External Snippet=] keeps its points in the file `tss:dataFile`, an IRI that is resolved against the IRI of the metadata document, so it may be relative (`<data.csv>`). `tss:format` says how to read it: `tss:CSV` or `tss:Parquet`. The metadata does not change the file: the file keeps its own column names and types, and the fields say how to read them. Each row of the file is a [=Point=].

## CSV ## {#csv}

A `tss:CSV` file is read as CSV [[RFC4180]]:

- `tss:delimiter` is the one character that separates the cells, such as `","` or `";"`. When it is not stated, it is `","`.
- `tss:encoding` is the text encoding of the file. When it is not stated, it is `"utf-8"`.
- `tss:header` says whether the first line names the columns. When it is not stated, the file has a header line.
- With a header line, a field's column is the column whose name is the field's `tss:columnName`. Without one (`tss:header false`), it is the column at the field's `tss:columnIndex`: the first column is 0.
- An empty cell is an [=Empty Value=].

<div class="example" id="example-csv-snippet">
An external snippet with a CSV file. It states its delimiter; the header line and the encoding are the defaults.

```turtle
ex:csv-snippet
    a tss:Snippet ;
    tss:embedded false ;
    tss:mutable false ;
    tss:timeDatatype xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:committedUntil "2024-06-01T00:05:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:from "2024-05-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/data.csv> ;
    tss:format tss:CSV ;
    tss:delimiter ";" ;
    tss:timeField ex:csv-time-field ;
    tss:key ex:csv-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:csv-time-field,
        ex:csv-value-field .

ex:csv-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:csv-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The first lines of the data file:

```
time;value
2024-05-01T00:00:00Z;12.5
2024-05-01T00:15:00Z;12.7
```
</div>

## Parquet ## {#parquet}

A `tss:Parquet` file is read by its column names: a field's column is the column whose name is its `tss:columnName`. `tss:delimiter`, `tss:encoding` and `tss:header` have no effect on it. The values keep their Parquet types in the file. To check a value against the field's datatype, the value is written in the lexical form of its Parquet type.

<div class="example" id="example-parquet-snippet">
The properties that make a snippet a Parquet snippet:

```turtle
ex:parquet-snippet
    tss:embedded false ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/data.parquet> ;
    tss:format tss:Parquet .
```
</div>

## Time grid ## {#time-grid}

An external snippet MAY give the time of each row as a step on a [=Time Grid=], instead of in a column of its own. It states the grid with `tss:timeBase`, the time of step 0, and `tss:timeStep`, the time between two steps, and MUST state both or neither. An embedded snippet MUST NOT state a time grid: every point states its own time.

- `tss:timeBase` is an `xsd:dateTime`. It anchors the grid and is not a bound of the [=Time Window=]: it stays the same when `tss:from` or `tss:until` change, so no row's time moves.
- `tss:timeStep` is an `xsd:duration` [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]] of more than zero, such as `"PT15M"` or `"P1M"`.

Step n is at `tss:timeBase` + n × `tss:timeStep`: each part of the step (years, months, days, hours, minutes and seconds) is multiplied by n, and the result is added to `tss:timeBase` as XML Schema adds a duration to a date and time [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]].

Note: With years or months, the steps are calendar steps, not equal lengths of time: a month has 28 to 31 days, and a day past the end of a month becomes its last day (2021-01-31 + P1M is 2021-02-28).

With a time grid, a column of the data file holds the step of each row. The snippet names the field of that column, the [=Step Field=], with exactly one `tss:stepField`, as `tss:timeField` names the time field:

- The step field MUST be one of the snippet's own fields (`tss:hasField`), and MUST NOT be its time field. Its `tss:datatype` MUST be `xsd:integer`.
- Every row MUST have a step: a whole number of 0 or more. No two rows may be at the same step.
- A row's time is the time of its step: that is the row's value for the time field. The data file has no column for the time field, so the time field MUST NOT state a `tss:columnName` or a `tss:columnIndex`.

A snippet without a time grid MUST NOT state a `tss:stepField`.

<div class="example" id="example-time-grid">
The readings of <a href="#example-embedded-snippet">the embedded example</a> in a CSV file, on a time grid of 15 minutes from 08:00. The column `step` holds the step of each row; the time field has no column.

```turtle
ex:grid-snippet
    a tss:Snippet ;
    tss:embedded false ;
    tss:mutable false ;
    tss:timeDatatype xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:committedUntil "2024-05-12T09:46:10Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/grid.csv> ;
    tss:format tss:CSV ;
    tss:timeBase "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:timeStep "PT15M"^^xsd:duration ;
    tss:timeField ex:grid-time-field ;
    tss:stepField ex:grid-step-field ;
    tss:key ex:grid-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:grid-time-field,
        ex:grid-step-field,
        ex:grid-value-field .

ex:grid-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:grid-step-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "step" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:integer .

ex:grid-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double .
```

The data file:

```
step,value
0,4810.5
1,4722.4
2,4751.9
5,4719.3
7,4783.6
```

Steps 3, 4 and 6 have no row. Step 5 is at 2024-05-12T08:00:00Z + 5 × PT15M, which is 2024-05-12T09:15:00Z.
</div>

## Value encoding ## {#value-encoding}

A field of an external snippet MAY state with `tss:valueEncoding` that the values in the data file are not the readings themselves. A field without a `tss:valueEncoding` states its readings: each value is its own [=Decoded Value=]. A field has at most one `tss:valueEncoding`, and the time field has none. The fields of an embedded snippet MUST NOT state `tss:valueEncoding` or `tss:baseline`: every point states the readings themselves.

This specification defines one value encoding, `tss:DeltaFromBaseline`: each value is the difference from the field's `tss:baseline`, and its decoded value is `tss:baseline` + the value. The sum is computed exactly on the decimal values of the two literals, and then read as the field's `tss:datatype`. The differences may be negative.

- A field with `tss:DeltaFromBaseline` MUST state exactly one `tss:baseline`: a number (`xsd:integer`, `xsd:decimal`, `xsd:double` or `xsd:float`) with the field's `tss:datatype`.
- A field without `tss:DeltaFromBaseline` MUST NOT state a `tss:baseline`.

`tss:minimum`, `tss:maximum` and the [=Stored Summary=] of the field describe the decoded values, not the differences, and so do the observations of the field ([[#expanding]]).

<div class="example" id="example-delta-from-baseline">
The first two readings of <a href="#example-embedded-snippet">the embedded example</a> in a CSV file whose value column states the difference from a baseline of 4750.

```turtle
ex:delta-snippet
    a tss:Snippet ;
    tss:embedded false ;
    tss:mutable false ;
    tss:timeDatatype xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:committedUntil "2024-05-12T09:46:10Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    tss:dataFile <https://example.org/delta.csv> ;
    tss:format tss:CSV ;
    tss:timeField ex:delta-time-field ;
    tss:key ex:delta-time-field ;
    tss:hasField ex:delta-time-field,
        ex:delta-value-field .

ex:delta-time-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "time" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:dateTime .

ex:delta-value-field
    a tss:Field ;
    tss:columnName "value" ;
    tss:datatype xsd:double ;
    tss:valueEncoding tss:DeltaFromBaseline ;
    tss:baseline "4750"^^xsd:double .
```

The data file:

```
time,value
2024-05-12T08:00:00Z,60.5
2024-05-12T08:15:00Z,-27.6
```

Their decoded values are 4750 + 60.5 = 4810.5 and 4750 + (-27.6) = 4722.4.
</div>

# Stored summaries # {#summaries}

A field MAY have one `tss:summary`: a node that holds numbers computed from the values of the field, such as how many values there are and their mean. A consumer can use them without reading the data. Every attribute of a summary is optional, and a summary may be empty.

The numbers of a summary cover the points in its own window: from its `tss:from` (included) until its `tss:until` (not included), normally the [=Time Window=] of the snippet. They are computed from the [=Decoded Values=] of the field ([[#value-encoding]]).

A summary states with `tss:missingValuePolicy` which values its numbers leave out. This specification defines one such policy, `tss:ExcludeEmptyValues`: an [=Empty Value=] is not a value, so the numbers leave it out. A text of only spaces is a value.

The attributes of a summary:

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:from</code>, <code>tss:until</code></td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td><td>the window of the summary</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code></td><td>IRI</td><td>which values the numbers leave out, such as <code>tss:ExcludeEmptyValues</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:count</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>how many points in the window have a value for the field</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:missing</code></td><td><code>xsd:integer</code>, 0 or more</td><td>how many points in the window have no value for the field; a time without a point is not counted</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:firstTime</code>, <code>tss:lastTime</code></td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td><td>the time of the first and of the last point that has a value</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedMinimum</code>, <code>tss:observedMaximum</code></td><td>number</td><td>the smallest and the largest value</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:mean</code></td><td>number</td><td>the unweighted arithmetic mean of the values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:median</code></td><td>number</td><td>the middle value, or the mean of the two middle values when their number is even</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:generatedAtTime</code></td><td><code>xsd:dateTime</code></td><td>when the numbers were computed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Only a numeric field ([[#datatypes]]) has `tss:observedMinimum`, `tss:observedMaximum`, `tss:mean` and `tss:median`. They include every value in the window, also values outside the allowed range of the field: the observed minimum and maximum do not replace `tss:minimum` and `tss:maximum`.

<div class="example" id="example-summary">
The summary of the value field of <a href="#example-embedded-snippet">the embedded example</a>:

```turtle
ex:value-field
    tss:summary [
        tss:from "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        tss:until "2024-05-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        tss:missingValuePolicy tss:ExcludeEmptyValues ;
        tss:count "5"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:missing "0"^^xsd:integer ;
        tss:firstTime "2024-05-12T08:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        tss:lastTime "2024-05-12T09:45:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
        tss:observedMinimum "4719.3"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:observedMaximum "4810.5"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:mean "4757.54"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:median "4751.9"^^xsd:double ;
        tss:generatedAtTime "2024-05-12T09:46:10Z"^^xsd:dateTime
    ] .
```
</div>

A summary is not refreshed by itself. Its `tss:generatedAtTime` says when it was computed, and a consumer can compute the same numbers from the data to check it. A [=Write=] that adds a point recomputes the summaries ([[#updating]]).

# Expanding a snippet into observations # {#expanding}

A snippet can be expanded into observations: every value of a [=Measured Field=] is one observation. An observation holds:

- the snippet: a link back to the `tss:Snippet` it comes from;
- the observed property: the field's `tss:observedProperty`;
- the sensor, the feature of interest and the unit: the field's `tss:sensor`, `tss:featureOfInterest` and `tss:unit`, when stated;
- the time: the time of its point, which in a data file with a [=Time Grid=] is the time of its step;
- the result: the [=Decoded Value=] of the value, as a literal with the field's `tss:datatype`, or with its `tss:language` for `rdf:langString`; a value without a value encoding keeps its text as written.

To expand a snippet:

1. Read the points: from the data file as [[#data-files]] says, or the nodes that link to the snippet with `tss:pointOf` ([[#points]]).
2. Keep the points whose time lies in the [=Time Window=]. A point without a time is an error.
3. For each point, and for each measured field for which the point has a value, make one observation: a new blank node that links back to the snippet.
4. A value that is not in the lexical form of its datatype is an error.
5. Add what the metadata says about the IRIs that the observations use, such as their observed properties and sensors: the concise bounded description of each, without the statements that use the TSS vocabulary.

Which RDF vocabulary the observations are written with is left to the implementer. The output holds no TSS vocabulary, and no description of the snippet or its fields: an observation only links back to its snippet. <a href="#example-observations">The observations of the embedded example</a> show the result for two points.

Note: The expansion does not keep every value: a field without `tss:observedProperty`, such as an identifier or a status, gives no observations.

# Adding points to a mutable snippet # {#updating}

The points of an [=Embedded Snippet=] with `tss:mutable true` may change. A [=Write=] that adds one point:

1. Checks the new point: every value is a literal in the lexical form of its field's datatype, and no other point has the same [=Key=].
2. Adds the new point: a new node with `tss:pointOf` and one triple for each of its values. The other points stay as they are.
3. Widens the [=Time Window=] when needed: `tss:from` becomes the earlier of `tss:from` and the earliest point, and `tss:until` the later of `tss:until` and the newest point plus one second, since `tss:until` is not in the window. A side that the snippet leaves open stays open.
4. Sets `tss:committedUntil` to the time of the write.
5. Recomputes every [=Stored Summary=] of the snippet from the points in the new window: its `tss:from` and `tss:until` become the window of the snippet, `tss:generatedAtTime` the time of the write, and every number it states is computed again. Its `tss:missingValuePolicy` stays.
6. Keeps the change only when the snippet still meets this specification.

<div class="example" id="example-write">
A new point for <a href="#example-embedded-snippet">the embedded example</a>:

```turtle
[] tss:pointOf ex:snippet ;
    ex:time-field "2024-05-12T10:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ex:value-field "4766.2"^^xsd:double .
```

The write keeps the window, since the new point lies inside it, and sets `tss:committedUntil` to the time of the write, not to `2024-05-12T10:00:00Z`.
</div>

An [=External Snippet=] is never changed this way: managing the data file of an external snippet is outside this specification. Its `tss:mutable` only tells consumers whether the file may change.

# Vocabulary overview # {#vocabulary}

*This section is informative.* All terms are in the namespace `https://w3id.org/tss#`.

<table class="data">
<thead>
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Kind</th><th>Used on</th><th>Section</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>tss:Snippet</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#the-snippet]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Field</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#fields]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Point</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#points]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Summary</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:Format</code>, <code>tss:MissingValuePolicy</code>, <code>tss:ValueEncoding</code></td><td>class</td><td>-</td><td>[[#data-files]], [[#summaries]], [[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:CSV</code>, <code>tss:Parquet</code></td><td>individual</td><td>value of <code>tss:format</code></td><td>[[#data-files]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:ExcludeEmptyValues</code></td><td>individual</td><td>value of <code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code></td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:DeltaFromBaseline</code></td><td>individual</td><td>value of <code>tss:valueEncoding</code></td><td>[[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:embedded</code>, <code>tss:mutable</code>, <code>tss:committedUntil</code></td><td>property</td><td>snippet</td><td>[[#storage]], [[#change]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeDatatype</code></td><td>property</td><td>snippet</td><td>[[#times]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:hasField</code>, <code>tss:timeField</code>, <code>tss:key</code></td><td>property</td><td>snippet</td><td>[[#time-and-key]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:from</code>, <code>tss:until</code></td><td>property</td><td>snippet, summary</td><td>[[#window]], [[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:dataFile</code>, <code>tss:format</code>, <code>tss:delimiter</code>, <code>tss:header</code>, <code>tss:encoding</code></td><td>property</td><td>external snippet</td><td>[[#data-files]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:timeBase</code>, <code>tss:timeStep</code>, <code>tss:stepField</code></td><td>property</td><td>external snippet</td><td>[[#time-grid]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:producedBy</code>, <code>tss:snippetDescription</code></td><td>property</td><td>snippet</td><td>[[#provenance]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:columnName</code>, <code>tss:columnIndex</code>, <code>tss:datatype</code>, <code>tss:language</code>, <code>tss:minimum</code>, <code>tss:maximum</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#fields]], [[#datatypes]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:valueEncoding</code>, <code>tss:baseline</code></td><td>property</td><td>field of an external snippet</td><td>[[#value-encoding]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:observedProperty</code>, <code>tss:unit</code>, <code>tss:sensor</code>, <code>tss:featureOfInterest</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#meaning]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:describesField</code>, <code>tss:fieldDescription</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#describing]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:summary</code></td><td>property</td><td>field</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:missingValuePolicy</code>, <code>tss:count</code>, <code>tss:missing</code>, <code>tss:firstTime</code>, <code>tss:lastTime</code>, <code>tss:observedMinimum</code>, <code>tss:observedMaximum</code>, <code>tss:mean</code>, <code>tss:median</code>, <code>tss:generatedAtTime</code></td><td>property</td><td>summary</td><td>[[#summaries]]</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tss:pointOf</code></td><td>property</td><td>point</td><td>[[#points]]</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

The vocabulary reuses `rdfs:label`, `rdfs:comment` and the XML Schema datatypes [[XMLSCHEMA11-2]].

<h2 class="no-num" id="conformance">Conformance</h2>

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "SHOULD" and "MAY" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [[!RFC2119]] [[!RFC8174]] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals.

Everything in this specification is normative, except the sections marked as informative, the examples, the notes and the issues.
